ult with multiple responses  to have the same people respond again  and again and again and so what we need  to do really is to make sure that every  person in this agency actually considers  response as a critical element of their  job there's a number of activities that  we're doing now to sort of move in that  direction I think there's a lot of  challenges that were overcoming we were  very siled the way we respond to various  threats before covid-19 the level of the  response needed really required us to  work across the agency to pull together  what I wanted that restructuring to  reflect is high visibility  accountability within the agency and  what are those key places that we really  need to be responsible so things like  our laboratory our office of science our  Public Health infrastructure and  Workforce I really want to decrease  layers of reporting decrease the  bureaucracy if you will  [Music]  we're in a bsl4 laboratory training  facility biosafety level four yes we  train people so that they can work  safely and effectively when they're  really dealing with the most pathogenic  agents that we have things like smallpox  Ebola when we looked at what's happened  in the pandemic there were really three  things we want to do better the first is  we can't have a test that goes out that  doesn't have a thorough enough quality  review the second thing we need to get  it as fast as possible to the private  sector and the third thing we want to  make sure we stay on top of the best  Technologies because we would like to  have tests like the tests that you get  in drug stores but to be as accurate as  the PCR test  I heard a lot of optimism from CDC staff  about their plans to move forward but  one potential obstacle is Congress  funding is a place we've seen Congress  reach a roadblock I mean what kind of  resources will it take to execute the  strategy that you've laid out one of the  challenges of CDC when I got here was  that we were working off a frail  und